=== Stage 1 - extraction (2026-11-02) ===
Source: J. A. Guccione, J. J. Guccione, R. Horruitiner, C. Valqui,
"Increasing the degree of a possible counterexample to the Jacobian Conjecture
from 100 to 108", arXiv:2204.14178v1. Prop. 4.3 is on PDF p.10; the LaTeX
source (arXiv e-print) has the proposition at lines 1000-1007 and the proof at
lines 1008-1311. Line numbers below are src.tex lines.

Statement (lines 1000-1007):
  If there is a counterexample in case (8,28), then there exist P,Q in L^(1)
  with [P,Q]=x^2 and either
    (1) N(P)={(0,0),(1,0),(8,14),(8,16),(0,8)},
        N(Q)={(0,0),(2,1),(12,21),(12,24),(0,12)};
    (2) N(P)={(0,0),(1,0),(8,14),(8,16)},
        N(Q)={(0,0),(2,1),(12,21),(12,24)}.
  Here L^(1)=K[x,x^-1,y] (line 656).

Every transformation used in the proof, in order:

T1. Swap. Line 1012:
    "we apply the automorphism phi_1 with phi_1(x)=y, phi_1(y)=x".
    This is applied while P,Q are still in K[x,y].
T2. Shear by x^-2. Line 1074:
    "if we apply the morphism given by phi(x)=x and phi(y)=y+lambda_1 x^-2".
    Used when Pred_P(1,0)=(1,-2).
T3. Shear by x^-3. Line 1079:
    "x -> x, y -> y+lambda x^-3 for adequate lambda".
    Line 1082: "x -> x, y -> y+alpha_1 x^-3".
T4. Shear by x^-4. Line 1132:
    "Apply then the automorphism phi_3 given by phi_3(x)=x,
     phi_3(y)=y+alpha x^-4", reducing the edge {(28,8),(0,1)} to
    {(28,8),(24,7)}.
T5. Monomial substitution / localization. Line 1229:
    "Apply the morphism phi with phi(x)=x^-1 and phi(y)=x^4 y. As in
     Proposition 4.1, this is not an automorphism and the chain rule gives
     [phi(P),phi(Q)] = -[P,Q] x^2."
    The analogous remark for Prop. 4.1 is lines 655-656:
    "Note that this is an automorphism of L^(1)=K[x,x^-1,y] but not of
     K[x,y]."
    Prop. 4.4 uses the same device at line 1395.

The proof also uses factorization data of leading forms (Corollary 7.4,
Proposition 2.5, Proposition 8.2) to choose the constants lambda_1, lambda,
alpha_1, alpha. These constants are read off from the original polynomial P,
not from the final normalized pair.

=== Stage 2 - invertibility of each step ===
T1 (swap): invertible on K[x,y]; it is its own inverse and det = -1.
  It is NOT an automorphism of L^(1): it sends the unit x to y, which is not
  a unit in K[x,x^-1,y]. This is the step that breaks the localization.
T2-T4 (shears y -> y + c x^-k, k=2,3,4): not maps K[x,y]->K[x,y] because of
  the negative exponent, but each is an automorphism of L^(1), with inverse
  y -> y - c x^-k. The constants c are not determined by the normalized pair.
T5 (phi): x -> x^-1, y -> x^4 y. Not a map K[x,y]->K[x,y]. It is an
  involution (phi^2=id), hence invertible as a rational map, and it is an
  automorphism of L^(1) by itself. It introduces x^-1. The paper explicitly
  says it is "not an automorphism" of K[x,y] (line 1229). det J(phi) = -x^2,
  so [phi(P),phi(Q)] = -[P,Q] x^2.
Composite A = T1 o T2 o T3 o T4 o T5:
  A(x,y) = (x^4 y + s(x^-1), x^-1), with s(x)=sum c_k x^-k, k in {2,3,4}.
  A is a morphism K[x,y] -> L^(1), not an automorphism of either ring.
  Its rational inverse is
    A^-1(u,v) = (v^-1, v^4 (u - s(v))).
  The first component v^-1 is not in L^(1)=K[u,u^-1,v] (it inverts the
  second variable). The paper gives no inverse formula and no description
  of the image A(K[x,y]) inside L^(1).
Which step introduces the denominator:
  Forward T5 introduces x^-1. In the inverse, undoing T5 introduces x^-1 in
  the first argument, which is allowed in L^(1). Undoing T1 (the swap) is the
  fatal step: it turns that x^-1 into y^-1, which is not in L^(1) and is not
  a polynomial. So the composite is not invertible on polynomial pairs; the
  inverse exists only as a rational map, defined on the image.
